When I was an undergraduate research assistant, I developed a multi-user table tennis scoring system using commodity wearable devices and smartphones. This system was a novel approach in that it didn’t require any third-party entity to receive data from both players. The score was automatically calculated by integrating multimodal signals, including accelerometer sensor, gravity sensor and audio signals, on each device and the devices were aware of the context of the game by passing messages to each other through WiFi-Direct. I was fascinated by a new value created by interaction of mobile devices, and it gave me a strong motivation to do in-depth research in context-aware computing based on mobile devices. Strong backgrounds of electronic engineering and mobile computing of the University of Texas at Austin is the main reason why I applied for Electrical and Computing Engineering Ph.D. program.

In my doctoral program, I would like to study cooperative mobile computing and online learning on mobile devices for context-awareness. From my academic and professional experiences, due to privacy issues, I have experienced many limitations to collect and utilize data in central servers. Moreover, even if machine learning has its potential, there have been many limitations for trained models to be sustainable in a continuous data stream. My research goal is to build privacy preserving and cooperative mobile systems and to research sustainable machine learning in a continuous data stream. In the University of Texas at Austin, Dr. Edison Thomaz and Dr. Christine Jullie are the most suitable professors for my goal. Dr. Edison Thomaz’s research on active learning with wearable devices is the best research fit for me in the aspect of continual machine learning on mobile devices. Dr. Christine Jullie’s research on federating learning is also the best research for me in the aspect of cooperative computing on mobile devices. 

I have prepared academically and professionally for graduate school. As an undergraduate researcher under the guidance of Dr. Ahyoung Choi, I researched multi-user activity recognition based on wearable devices and developed real-time monitoring systems for human signals. My research on multi-user activity recognition – specifically multi-user table tennis scoring system – helped me to enhance problem solving skills with understanding of wireless network and message passing architecture. A big challenge of the research was how the system could be designed without any third-party entity and Internet network. I proposed synchronized message passing architecture on mobile devices with WiFi -Direct. This architecture improved the usability of the system in case of no Internet and no additional devices to collect data and process algorithms. I published multiple papers and applied for a patent related to this research.   
My project about real-time systems for human signals improved my basic engineering skills related to building backend systems with Transmission Control Protocol servers, web servers and databases. For example, I was involved in the project of an unobtrusive tele-monitoring system for sleep and respiration. I developed TCP servers and databases for 1 kHz data from sensors and placed queues for data buffering. The web servers took and displayed sensor data from buffers in real time. I kept working on this project even after I graduated my university and got a job, and this shows my passion and responsibility for my research. The project was published in a journal, IEEE Access.

In Samsung Electronics, I was involved in multiple projects related to machine learning for various structures of big data. In the first year, I analyzed time series data and developed a reinforcement learning model to manage emulation resources for digital design. As there were few references on the project, the project seemed to be unsuccessful. However, I didn't give up and looked up references from other domains. Fortunately, I found application of reinforcement learning on adaptive congestion control in the network domain and developed a Double Deep Q Network model. As a result, the proposed model increased resource utilization by 20%. In recognition of the company's cost reduction and publishing paper at the top conference, I received the Best Paper Award from my company. Nowadays, I develop a prioritization algorithm based on clustering terabytes of log data from digital design simulation.
I have also built distributed machine learning pipelines for a continuous data stream. Before my project, my team struggled with high latency of model inference time and tedious tasks for machine learning development and deployment. To solve these challenges, I proposed an efficient and scalable system with in-memory machine learning models and pipeline automation. This architecture decreased model inference time from 40 seconds to 1 second on average and reduced the number of tedious machine learning development and deployment steps by 80%.

After my PhD, I will become a professor in the university where I studied. It was a place where I had a lot of good experience and expertise under the guidance of great professors when I knew nothing about Computer Science. I want to go back there and be a good advisor and researcher like the professors. Furthermore, I would like to make a leading laboratory in South Korea by conducting research on ubiquitous computing, which has not been actively researched in my country.

